In the subdirectory tqmesh, you find a partial copy of TQMesh by Florian Setzwein, a header-only
two-dimensional mesh generator for triangular and quadrilateral elements.

See tqmesh/LICENSE.md for the original licence of TQMesh (MIT).

TQMesh is hosted at https://github.com/FloSewn/TQMesh

Copied from TQMesh 1.4.0, commit d77ad3084d4c8746ef99bd03c2dd156532dd7178 (31st July 2026).

TQMesh is exposed to python by src/nanobind/tqmesh/, which is the only place in pyoomph that
includes these headers. Both are dropped from the build by -DPYOOMPH_HAS_TQMESH=OFF.

##########################################################################
Following changes were made with respect to the original TQMesh files:
These changes are indicated by a comment start with "//FOR PYOOMPH"
##########################################################################
1) Only the header-only library itself was copied, i.e. TQMesh's src/algorithm and the part of its
   src/utils that the former requires. Left out were TQMesh's command line application (src/app),
   its examples and unit tests (src/examples, src/tests), its build system, its documentation and
   its bundled copy of exprtk (src/extern_libs), which only serves the command line application to
   parse mesh size functions from its input files - pyoomph passes a python callable instead.

   From src/utils, the following files were not copied, since nothing in src/algorithm or in the
   remaining src/utils refers to them:

   ParaReader.h, StringOps.h:  parser for the input files of the command line application
   Testing.h:                  assertion macros of the unit tests
   Timer.h:                    timing utility used by the examples
   Matrix.h:                   unused

2) Following files have been changed and the positions marked with "//FOR PYOOMPH"

CppUtils.h (Changed on 31st July 2026):
Removed the includes of Timer.h, Testing.h and ParaReader.h, which are not copied (see above).

Helpers.h (Changed on 31st July 2026):
Made ASSERT/assert_msg and TERMINATE throw a std::runtime_error rather than calling assert() (a
debug build), doing nothing at all (a release build) or exit(EXIT_FAILURE). Both conditions do fire
on input that is merely unsuitable rather than wrong - a mesh size function returning zero, or a
quad layer requested between positions not connected by boundary edges - and inside a python
session neither aborting the process nor carrying on with a violated invariant is acceptable: the
latter is what turned a failed quad layer into a segmentation fault. The assertions are hence also
active in release builds now.

QuadTree.h (Changed on 31st July 2026):
Replaced the ASSERT in ~QuadTree() by a plain null check, since throwing from a destructor (see
Helpers.h above) would call std::terminate.

3) Following file was added, i.e. it is NOT part of TQMesh:

TQMeshConfig.h:
Several TQMesh headers include <TQMeshConfig.h> unconditionally. Upstream generates it from
auxiliary/TQMeshConfig.h.in with configure_file() in its own CMakeLists.txt, which pyoomph does not
run - it compiles the vendored headers directly. The replacement only reproduces the version
macros; see the comment in the file itself.
